<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zubarah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Al Zubarah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zubarah is a ruined town on the north-west coast of Qatar. Settled in the 18th century by clans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bani-utbah">Bani Utbah</a>, including the Al <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa">Khalifa</a>, it grew into a thriving centre of pearling and trade, and in 1783 the Al Khalifa crossed from there to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>. Claims to it caused disputes between Bahrain and Qatar until 2001. Its excavated remains became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2013.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zubarah.jpg" alt="Zubarah" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rafeek Qatar · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zubara_Fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
